that $a,b,c\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "We perform the multiplication under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6{a}^{3}b}\\cdot \\sqrt{15abc} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{6{a}^{3}b\\cdot 15abc} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{90{a}^{4}{b}^{2}c} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{9{a}^{4}{b}^{2}\\cdot 10c} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(3{a}^{2}b)^{2}\\cdot 10c} & = \\\\ 3\\cdot |{a}^{2}|\\cdot |b|\\cdot \\sqrt{10c}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have that $|{a}^{2}|={a}^{2}$ and $|b|=b.$  Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot |{a}^{2}|\\cdot |b|\\cdot \\sqrt{10c} & = \\\\ 3{a}^{2}b\\sqrt{10c} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563675 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184321 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de2b7926-fa13-5636-9682-8fa382336be7" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Cube Root Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f9d67ae-479b-5212-a32d-ea72ac199fdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\sqrt[3]{s{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562975} {:answer/id #uuid "3121334e-b5ff-5d7f-b931-c4cc6aff67e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{14st}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563057} {:answer/id #uuid "44050778-0a11-5b3d-8de9-552e09f1598c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\sqrt[3]{28s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563097} {:answer/id #uuid "66a4a5e4-546c-53dd-b661-09d0f6e93243" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\sqrt[3]{st}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563184} {:answer/id #uuid "e708cbcb-204a-515f-a2ee-8880e52ab193" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{28s{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e708cbcb-204a-515f-a2ee-8880e52ab193" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{28s{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563454} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a3551f7-6554-57bf-8d88-2777fb8978a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "41a2ad72-53df-54b1-8f6d-95041c4754db" :question/math-academy-id "q-141851" :question/problem "$\\sqrt[3]{4s}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{7{t}^{2}}=$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the product rule for radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{4s}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{7{t}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{4s\\cdot 7{t}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{28s{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563692 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184320 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fcba20f-b43b-558d-bd84-d76816d3186b" :knowledge-point/title "Combining Radical Expressions Using the Rules of Absolute Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02d5136f-8e3c-524a-ae1b-d2c7d6281429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36{b}^{2}\\sqrt{a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562943} {:answer/id #uuid "4ee323e3-08d6-546b-8935-72052398ce78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36a{b}^{2}\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563124} {:answer/id #uuid "9a2ab45f-3b8e-59d8-ae0d-a84bcaf91d90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36{b}^{2}\\sqrt{2a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563292} {:answer/id #uuid "cfba2d73-242a-5b18-a815-bc4b2611b506" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6a{b}^{2}\\sqrt{11a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563401} {:answer/id #uuid "fc68b7fb-a728-5929-84b3-caef82248a8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18{b}^{2}\\sqrt{a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563512}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a2ab45f-3b8e-59d8-ae0d-a84bcaf91d90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36{b}^{2}\\sqrt{2a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563292} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a8b84a5-9da7-5fc7-9d58-31f7e5a5ff16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "60bf46ec-c41d-5bd2-93b6-b056ca651773" :question/math-academy-id "q-3077" :question/problem "$2\\sqrt{8a{b}^{3}}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{9b}=$" :question/worked-solution "We perform the multiplication under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{8a{b}^{3}}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{9b} & = \\\\ 2\\cdot 3\\sqrt{8a{b}^{3}\\cdot 9b} & = \\\\ 6\\sqrt{72a{b}^{4}} & = \\\\ 6\\sqrt{36{b}^{4}\\cdot 2a} & = \\\\ 6\\sqrt{(6{b}^{2})^{2}\\cdot 2a} & = \\\\ 6\\sqrt{(6{b}^{2})^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2a} & = \\\\ 6\\cdot |6{b}^{2}|\\cdot \\sqrt{2a} & = \\\\ 6\\cdot |6|\\cdot |{b}^{2}|\\cdot \\sqrt{2a} & = \\\\ 6\\cdot 6\\cdot |{b}^{2}|\\cdot \\sqrt{2a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot 6\\cdot |{b}^{2}|\\cdot \\sqrt{2a} & = \\\\ 36\\cdot {b}^{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2a} & = \\\\ 36{b}^{2}\\sqrt{2a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
